Mitch, did you read the whole article, or just my pseudo-fortune?

O.K. maybe the article I posted wasn't clear.  The paragraph that I was
responding to went like this:

In article <1990Jul9.105655.11441@irisa.fr> roudier@irisa.fr (Francois Roudier)
writes:
| I've just noticed a very surprising bug : today (9/07/90) is a "lucky" day,
| but my luck decreased drastically, without apparent reasons. Finally, in
| wizard mode, I discovered that today, when I save and restore a game, my luck
| is decreased by 1 ! ( if it is already <= 0 ). That's rather curious !

What Francois referred to as a "lucky" day was a night with a full moon.
There is a bug in the code that deals with such nights.  If you start
up a game under a full moon, you do indeed get one extra luck point.
The problem occurs when, for some reason, your luck goes down to 0 during
the course of your "full moon" game.  (It should stay at +1, if you do
absolutely nothing wrong).  If you save your game at this point, and then
restore it, you get an EXTRA point taken away, and you now have -1 luck!
If you save and restore again, you will have -2 luck!  And so on.

This is definitely a bug, and an unacceptable one in my opinion!  It
should be corrected immediately.  It is very hard to know what your
luck is...especially if you do not know everything that affects you luck
(do any of us?).  Thus, you can't always be sure that it is safe to play
your game under a full moon (unless you want to leave it running for
several days--not saving and restoring the game--that is impracticle
for most people).  Hence my pseudo-fortune:
"They say that you should avoid a full moon like the Plague"
I know I will!  That is, until this bug gets fixed.

